========================================================================================
                            AT Command Utility - Usage Guide
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 WARNING: Use with caution! If you are not sure what a command does DO NOT EXECUTE IT.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Usage:
  atcmd [options] "AT-COMMAND"

Options:
  -a, --at             AT command
  -d, --delay <ms>     Delay in milliseconds (default: 600)
  -o, --output <file>  Save output (only /tmp/atcmd/)
  -n, --no-log         Disable syslog logging

  -S, --smart-qnwlock  Apply SMART QNWLOCK procedure:
                       - Queries current cell-lock status (AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"?)
                       - If lock present: removes it (AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",0),
                         then restarts modem via soft CFUN (AT+CFUN=0/1),
                         restarts WAN, and checks LTE connectivity.
                       - If no lock: returns rc=13, no CFUN budget consumed.
                       Accepts AT+QNWLOCK command as parameter.

  -C, --smart-cfun     Execute SMART CFUN procedure (AT+CFUN=0 + AT+CFUN=1)
                       with modem restart, WAN restart, and LTE connectivity check.
                       Does not accept additional AT commands.
                       Preserves any existing cell-lock configuration.

  -Z, --sanity-check   Check modem is genuinely responsive (AT+CIMI)
                       Returns 0 if healthy, 1 if zombie state detected

  -h, --help, -help, help                      Show this help (brief usage)
  --help-full, -help-full, help-full, full     Show full documentation with examples

Notes:

  - If -a/--at is omitted, the first non-option argument is treated as the AT command.
  - atcmd -C exits with code 11 if the daily CFUN invocation limit has been reached (15/24h window).
  - The default AT port is /dev/ttyUSB2.
  - The WAN interface name is configured via wan_iface at the top of the script.
  - This utility is not compatible with ModemManager.
  - Output files are restricted to /tmp/atcmd/ for safety reasons.
  - AT commands are blocked if /tmp/watchdog/atcmd_block exists.
  - Script exits with error (exit 5) if the interface defined in wan_iface
    is not found via ubus at startup. Edit wan_iface at the top of the script
    to match the LTE interface name defined in /etc/config/network.

Warning:

  - Many AT commands modify persistent modem settings (NVRAM).
  - Some commands may cause loss of network connectivity or remote access.
  - Some commands require a longer delay (-d) to return complete output.
  - Commands that do not return 'OK' may be reported as failed.
  - Commands and responses are logged to syslog by default (use -n to disable).

Warning - special commands:
  - Many AT commands modify persistent modem settings (NVRAM).
  - Using -S with AT+QNWLOCK will restart the modem and restart WAN, then verify LTE automatically.
  - Using -C performs a soft CFUN reset (AT+CFUN=0/1), preserving any cell-lock configuration.
  - The CFUN operation counter is enforced daily to preserve modem flash lifespan.

Daily CFUN limit: 15 operations per day (uptime-based 24h window).
Return codes:
  0  = success
  11 = daily CFUN limit reached (escalation aborted)
  12 = critical failure (modem unresponsive, zombie state, etc)
  13 = atcmd -S: no active cell-lock (fallback to atcmd -C recommended)

Return code 13 (no cell-lock) is not a failure; it informs the watchdog that
removal is unnecessary and -C can be used directly to save CFUN budget.

Examples:

# Basic AT Commands
atcmd 'AT';           # Basic communication test (equivalent to: atcmd -a 'AT')
atcmd 'ATI';          # Show device information (equivalent to: atcmd -a 'ATI')
atcmd 'AT+CVERSION';  # Show firmware version
atcmd 'AT+QGMR?';     # Show extended firmware version
atcmd 'ATE1';         # Enable the "echo" mode or use 'ATE0' to disable it
atcmd 'AT&W';         # Save all current settings to non-volatile memory (retains them after reboot)

# PDP Context Management
atcmd 'AT+CGDCONT?';      # Show defined PDP contexts
atcmd 'AT+CGACT?';        # Show active PDP contexts
atcmd 'AT+CGACT=0,1';     # Deactivate PDP context 1
atcmd 'AT+CGATT?';        # Check if modem is attached to network

# SIM Status
atcmd 'AT+CPIN?';                # Check SIM status (READY, PIN required, etc.)
atcmd 'AT+CCID';                 # Show SIM CCID (only works if SIM is inserted)
atcmd 'AT+CLCK="SC",0,"1234"'    # Disable SIM PIN (replace 1234 with actual PIN)
atcmd 'AT+CLCK="SC",1,"1234"'    # Enable SIM PIN
atcmd 'AT+CPWD="SC","OLD","NEW"' # Change SIM PIN

# Network Information
atcmd 'AT+CGPADDR=1';     # Show current IP address
atcmd 'AT+CSQ';           # Show signal quality [ atcmd -d 500 -a 'AT+CSQ'; # Delay 500ms ]
atcmd 'AT+QCSQ';          # Show signal quality (RSRP, SINR, RSRQ) - format: +QCSQ: "LTE",<rssi>,<rsrp>,<sinr>,<rsrq>
atcmd 'AT+QRSRP';         # Show RSRP signal level
atcmd 'AT+QSINR';         # Show SINR value (format: <pcc>,<scc1>,<scc2>,<scc3>)
atcmd 'AT+CEREG?';        # Check LTE network registration
atcmd 'AT+COPS?';         # Query current registered operator and access technology (7=LTE, 2=UMTS, 0=GSM)
atcmd 'AT+QNWINFO';       # Show current network info (mode, operator, band, channel)

# Primary Cell Info
atcmd 'AT+QENG="servingcell"';      # Show serving cell info
atcmd 'AT+QNETINFO="servingcell"';  # Show current network info

# Neighboring Cells Info
atcmd 'AT+QENG="neighbourcell"';    # Show neighbouring cells

# Carrier Aggregation
atcmd 'AT+QCAINFO';                 # Show carrier aggregation info, Requires active data traffic; otherwise returns "OK" only

# SMS Storage / Voice Function
atcmd 'AT+CPMS?';                                # Show current SMS storage
atcmd 'AT+CPMS="ME","ME","ME"'; atcmd 'AT+CSAS'; # Set and save SMS storage to ME
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="call_control",1,1';              # Disable voice call support

# LTE Bands
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",0,5,0,1';                # Use LTE bands 1+3 only
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",0,45,0,1';               # Use LTE bands 1+3+7 only
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",0,80045,0,1';            # Use LTE bands 1+3+7+20 only
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="band",8d0,1A0080800D5,0,1';    # Enable all supported LTE bands (EG18-EA)

# USB / Network Interface
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="usbnet"';                             # Show USB network type (0=qmi, 1=ecm, 2=mbim) ** WARNING: mode 3=RNDIS is NOT supported on Linux, only Windows **
atcmd 'AT+QCFG="usbcfg",0x2C7C,0x0512,1,1,1,1,1,1,0'; # Enable ADB interface

# Modem Control
atcmd 'AT+QTEMP';        # Show modem temperature
atcmd 'AT+QPOWD=1';      # Power off the LTE modem - WARNING: modem will NOT restart automatically, may cause permanent loss of remote connectivity if no watchdog is in place
atcmd 'AT+CFUN=1,1';     # Hard reboot modem and enable radio (full reset). 
                         # WARNING: hard reset removes any cell-lock as a side effect 
                         #          (cell-locks are volatile in modem RAM).
                         # WARNING: only for emergency scenarios; regular recovery 
                         #          should use atcmd -C or -S instead.

========================================================================================
STANDARD AT+QNWLOCK COMMANDS
========================================================================================

atcmd 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"';            # Show current cell lock status
atcmd 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",0';          # Disable cell lock without check or fallback
atcmd 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",1,1650,100'; # Single cell lock without check or fallback

========================================================================================
SMART QNWLOCK EXAMPLES (-S)
========================================================================================

# SMART QNWLOCK performs the following steps automatically:
# 1. Queries current cell-lock status (AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"?)
# 2. Sends the AT command passed by the user (if it specifies a cell lock > 0).
# 3. Restarts modem via soft CFUN (AT+CFUN=0â1).
# 4. Restarts WAN interface.
# 5. Checks LTE connectivity via standard diagnostics.
# 6. On failure: 
#    - Logs the failure
#    - Fallback procedure is NOT attempted with -S alone
#    - (Fallback logic is delegated to the watchdog_mbim daemon)

# SMART QNWLOCK only executes the full procedure if cell-locks are active 
# (AT+QNWLOCK value >= 1). If no lock is present, it returns rc=13 without 
# consuming CFUN budget.

# Smart QNWLOCK examples
atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g"';                            # Execute standard command only
atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",0';                          # Execute SMART only if cell-locks are active
atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",1,1650,100';                 # Monte Bondone Cima Palon 230131 or 231131
atcmd -S 'AT+QNWLOCK="common/4g",3,1650,100,1650,65,1500,50'; # Monte Bondone Cima Palon 230131 or 231131

========================================================================================
SMART CFUN EXAMPLES (-C)
========================================================================================

# SMART CFUN performs the following steps automatically:
# 1. Restarts modem via soft CFUN (AT+CFUN=0/1)
#    NOTE: Soft reset preserves existing cell-lock configuration
# 2. Restarts WAN interface.
# 3. Checks LTE connectivity via standard diagnostics.
# 4. Returns 0 on success, 11 if daily limit reached, 12 on failure.

# Smart CFUN is used for general connectivity recovery without 
# touching cell-lock configuration. Use -C when cell-lock 
# management is not needed (remove_cell_lock=0 in watchdog_mbim).

# Smart CFUN examples
atcmd -C                    # Soft CFUN restart, preserve cell-lock
atcmd -C                    # Can be repeated (respects daily limit)

========================================================================================
HARD RESET vs SOFT RESET
========================================================================================

AT+CFUN=1,1 (Hard Reset):
  - Full modem reboot
  - Clears modem RAM state
  - Removes cell-locks (volatile, not NV memory)
  - Reloads firmware from flash
  - Use only in emergencies (watchdog escalation does NOT use this)

AT+CFUN=0 + AT+CFUN=1 (Soft Reset):
  - Radio power cycle only
  - Preserves modem RAM (NVRAM untouched, cell-locks preserved)
  - Standard recovery sequence in atcmd -C and atcmd -S

The watchdog_mbim daemon uses soft reset (-C and -S) for regular recovery.
Hard reset (AT+CFUN=1,1) is NOT used in normal escalation flows.
